<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">Morava (river)</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Morava, called the March in German, is a river of central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> that rises in the north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a> and flows south about 350 kilometres through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, to which it gives its name, before joining the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>. Its lower course forms the borders between the Czech Republic, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, and its valley was the centre of Great Moravia in the 9th century.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/morava-river.jpg" alt="Morava (river)" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Palickap · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Marchegg,_March_(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The Morava rises on the slope of the Králický Sněžník mountain at an elevation of 1,371 m, close to the border with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>. Its total length is 352 km, of which 269.4 km lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, and it carries an average of 110 cubic metres of water a second from a drainage area of 26,658 square kilometres. It is a lowland river and, unusually for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, a blackwater river. Its valley forms the Upper Morava Valley and the Lower Morava Valley in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, the Marchfeld in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-austria">Lower Austria</a> and the Záhorie Lowland in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>, which together make up a large part of the Vienna Basin.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/olomouc">Olomouc</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kromeriz">Kroměříž</a>, Otrokovice, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uherske-hradiste">Uherské Hradiště</a>, Veselí nad Moravou and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hodonin">Hodonín</a> stand on the river, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brno">Brno</a> lies within its basin. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaya">Thaya</a>, its longest tributary, joins it at the tripoint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czech-republic">Czech Republic</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slovakia">Slovakia</a>. The river enters the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> just below Devín Castle on the outskirts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bratislava">Bratislava</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The root of the name, mor-, comes from a Proto-Indo-European word for water or marsh. The river was first recorded as Maraha in a deed of 892, while the oldest record of the land of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moravia">Moravia</a>, from 822, is older than that of the river. The German name March derives from the Slavic one.</p><p>People have lived on the banks for a very long time: Stillfried, on the Austrian side, had a settlement 30,000 years ago, and farming began in the valley about 7,000 years ago. Below the confluence with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thaya">Thaya</a> the river is one of the oldest national boundaries still in use in continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Around 800 it was the eastern boundary of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carolingian-empire">Carolingian Empire</a> with the Avar Khaganate, and from the 10th century it divided the imperial marcha orientalis, later the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a>, from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-hungary">Kingdom of Hungary</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cold-war">Cold War</a> it formed part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-curtain">Iron Curtain</a> between Austria and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/czechoslovakia">Czechoslovakia</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The river links the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> valley with the plains of northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. Its gentle slope makes it meander and flood, and its floodplains are among the most biologically diverse in Europe, with some 12,000 species recorded. Much of the river, especially downstream from Litovel, was regulated in the 20th century, with the loss of floodplain. The central part has kept much of its natural character.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Morava (river)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Moravia, the land along the <b>Morava</b> river between Bohemia, Hungary, Austria and Silesia, had been the core of Great Moravia in the ninth century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/margraviate-of-moravia">Margraviate of Moravia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After Maroboduus and his successor Catualda were driven out in 19, their followers were settled by Rome north of the Danube between the March (<b>Morava</b>) and Váh rivers.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-vannius">Kingdom of Vannius</a></li></ul></section>
